# Line endings — normalise in the repository, check out LF everywhere.
#
# This repo is authored on macOS/Linux but cloned onto Windows hosts, where
# Git-for-Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` at *system* scope. Without this
# file that setting rewrites every file with CRLF on checkout, which breaks
# shebangs (`#!/bin/bash\r` is not an interpreter), Docker entrypoints, and any
# byte-exact comparison. The failure is silent at checkout and loud at runtime.
#
# This file is authoritative and travels with the repo, so correctness no
# longer depends on any individual machine's git configuration.
#
# `text=auto` (not bare `text`) is deliberate: it preserves the LF guarantee for
# text files while letting git's binary detection protect any binary that is not
# yet enumerated below — which matters in a repo that will accumulate model
# weights and checkpoints.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.whl binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.dll binary
*.a binary
*.o binary
*.pyc binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary

# Model weights and serialized tensors — large, binary, never text.
*.gguf binary
*.onnx binary
*.npz binary
*.npy binary
*.pt binary
*.pth binary
*.bin binary
*.safetensors binary
*.ckpt binary
*.h5 binary

# Windows-native scripts require CRLF — cmd.exe and PowerShell will not parse LF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
